#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:1
msgid "Modelling mechanobiological processes"
msgstr "Mechanobiological Processes のモデル化"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:3
msgid "Origin"
msgstr "作成について"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:5
msgid ""
"This tutorial was authored by Claire Pritchard, Guillaume Stirnemann, and "
"Glen M. Hocky in October 2024."
msgstr ""
"このチュートリアルは、2024年10月に Claire Pritchard、Guillaume Stirnemann、Glen M. Hocky "
"によって作成された。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:7
msgid "Aims"
msgstr "目的"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:9
msgid ""
"This Masterclass explains how mechanical forces can be modeled using PLUMED,"
" and how to compute their result using free energy and kinetics "
"calculations."
msgstr ""
"この Masterclass では、PLUMED で Mechanical Forces をモデル化し、自由エネルギーや Kinetics "
"の計算でその効果を求める方法を説明する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:11
msgid "Objectives"
msgstr "到達目標"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:13
msgid "The objectives of this Masterclass are:"
msgstr "この Masterclass の到達目標は次のとおりである。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:14
msgid "Learn how to apply a constant force in PLUMED;"
msgstr "PLUMED で Constant Force を加える方法を学ぶ。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:15
msgid "See how transition rates change with different forces."
msgstr "Force によって Transition Rates がどう変わるかを調べる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:17
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr "前提条件"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:19
msgid ""
"We assume that the person that will follow this tutorial is familiar with "
"the Linux terminal, Gromacs and basic functionality of PLUMED."
msgstr "Linux の Terminal、GROMACS、および PLUMED の基本機能に習熟していることを前提とする。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:21
msgid "Familiarity with python and matplotlib is recommended for plotting"
msgstr "描画のため、Python と matplotlib に習熟していることを勧める。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:23
msgid "Setting up PLUMED"
msgstr "PLUMED のセットアップ"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:25
msgid ""
"We will use GROMACS, PLUMED, and PLUMED's pesmd function to perform the "
"calculations. Conda packages with the software required for this class have "
"been prepared and you can install them following the instructions in [this "
"link](https://github.com/plumed/masterclass-2022)."
msgstr ""
"計算には GROMACS、PLUMED、および PLUMED の pesmd を使う。必要なソフトウェアの Conda "
"パッケージが用意されているので、[こちら](https://github.com/plumed/masterclass-2022)の手順でインストールできる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:28
msgid ""
"The data needed to run the exercises of this Masterclass can be found on "
"[GitHub](https://github.com/hockyg/plumed-tutorial-force2)."
msgstr ""
"この Masterclass の演習に必要なデータは [GitHub](https://github.com/hockyg/plumed-"
"tutorial-force2) にある。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:30
msgid ""
"You can clone this repository locally on your machine if you want, but "
"specific data will be linked to below."
msgstr "必要ならリポジトリを手元に clone できるが、個別のデータへのリンクも以下に示す。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:32
msgid "Background"
msgstr "背景"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:34
msgid "Force basics"
msgstr "Force の基本"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:36
msgid ""
"A force along some direction here is defined as the negative gradient of the"
" potential energy along that direction."
msgstr "ここでは、ある方向の Force を、その方向に沿う Potential Energy の負の Gradient と定義する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:38
msgid ""
"A constant force $F$ on a scalar collective variable $Q(\\vec{X})$ therefore"
" is a simple addition to the system's energy function."
msgstr ""
"したがって、Scalar Collective Variable $Q(\\vec{X})$ に加える Constant Force $F$ は、系の "
"Energy Function に単純な項を追加して表せる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:44
msgid ""
"Notice that, because of the negative sign, a postive value of $F$ results in"
" a lower energy for large values of $Q$, meaning $F\\gt0$ corresponds to a "
"\"pulling\" force."
msgstr ""
"負号があるため、$F$ が正なら $Q$ が大きいほど Energy が低くなる。つまり $F\\gt0$ は引っ張る方向の Force に対応する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:46
msgid ""
"A mechanical force would often in reality would correspond to pulling apart "
"two atoms, and so $Q$ would often be a simple distance coordinate."
msgstr "実際の Mechanical Force は2つの原子を引き離すことに対応する場合が多く、$Q$ に単純な距離座標を用いることが多い。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:48
msgid ""
"Note however, that other quantities could be used, such as an area which "
"would mean $F$ corresponds to a pressure."
msgstr "ただし、面積など他の量も使える。その場合、$F$ は Pressure に対応する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:50
msgid ""
"Dimensional analysis implies that the units of $F$ must be [Energy]/[Q]."
msgstr "Dimensional Analysis から、$F$ の単位は [Energy]/[Q] でなければならない。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:52
msgid ""
"The effect of constant force can be assessed using any enhanced sampling "
"method."
msgstr "Constant Force の効果は、任意の Enhanced Sampling 法で評価できる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:54
msgid "The work done by the bias in this case is very simple:"
msgstr "この場合、Bias がする Work は非常に単純である。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:60
msgid ""
"Constant forces can be applied in PLUMED with the SLOPE keyword of the "
"RESTRAINT bias."
msgstr "PLUMED では、RESTRAINT Bias の SLOPE キーワードで Constant Forces を適用できる。"

# Method name, software identifier or literal command intentionally preserved.
#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:62
msgid "Steered MD"
msgstr "Steered MD"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:64
msgid ""
"Steered molecular dynamics (SMD) is one way of pulling on a molecular "
"coordinate, and has a connection to experiments done where a molecule is "
"attached via a \"spring\" to an object such as an optical tweezer or AFM "
"tip. To represent this in simulation, instead of applying a constant force, "
"we impose a Harmonic restraint on $Q$ with a center that moves:"
msgstr ""
"Steered Molecular Dynamics（SMD）は、分子座標を引っ張る方法の1つである。分子を Spring を介して Optical "
"Tweezers や AFM Tip に結び付ける実験と関係している。シミュレーションでは、Constant Force の代わりに、中心が移動する "
"Harmonic Restraint を $Q$ に加えて表す。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:70
msgid ""
"Typically $Q_0(t)$ would move linearly, with $Q_0(t) = Q_0(0)-\\lambda\\ t$ "
"although that is not a requirement."
msgstr "必須ではないが、通常 $Q_0(t)$ は $Q_0(t) = Q_0(0)-\\lambda\\ t$ のように線形に移動する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:72
msgid ""
"At any given time, the force along $Q$ from the moving bias is given as:"
msgstr "任意の時刻に、移動する Bias が $Q$ に沿って加える Force は次のようになる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:78
msgid ""
"This force is positive (pulling) when $Q_0(t)$ is bigger than $Q$, and it "
"can get very large if the spring moves quickly to larger values."
msgstr ""
"$Q_0(t)$ が $Q$ より大きいとき、この Force は正、すなわち引っ張る方向となる。Spring が大きい値へ速く動くと、Force "
"は非常に大きくなることがある。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:80
msgid ""
"SMD is implemented in PLUMED using the MOVINGRESTRAINT bias, and the work is"
" computed automatically:"
msgstr "SMD は PLUMED の MOVINGRESTRAINT Bias で実装され、Work も自動的に計算される。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:86
msgid "where,"
msgstr "ここで、"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:92
msgid "Dynamics from Infrequent Metadynamics"
msgstr "Infrequent Metadynamics から求める Dynamics"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:94
msgid ""
"To compute the rate constant of unbinding in a standard molecular dynamics "
"simulation, one would run many simulations starting from the bound pose, and"
" then compute the mean time of escape, by averaging the times at which "
"unbinding is deemed to have occured, $\\tau=\\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^N "
"t^{unbind}_i$ (this assumes every simulation ends in an unbinding event)."
msgstr ""
"通常の MD で Unbinding の Rate Constant を計算するには、Bound Pose "
"から多数のシミュレーションを開始し、Unbinding が起きたと判定される時刻を平均して、平均 Escape Time を求める。すなわち "
"$\\tau=\\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^N t^{unbind}_i$ である。これは、すべてのシミュレーションが "
"Unbinding Event で終わることを仮定している。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:96
msgid "In this case, the unbinding rate constant is $k_{off}=1/\\tau$."
msgstr "この場合、Unbinding Rate Constant は $k_{off}=1/\\tau$ となる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:99
msgid ""
"One way to compute dynamics from biased simulations is [Infrequent "
"Metadynamics](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.111.230602). In this "
"approach, we apply bias with a slow PACE so that the transition over the "
"barrier to escape is not likely to be biased. In this case with some other "
"assumptions, $\\tau = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha_i \\t_i$, where"
msgstr ""
"Biased シミュレーションから Dynamics を求める方法の1つが、[Infrequent "
"Metadynamics](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.111.230602) "
"である。この方法では、Escape Barrier を越える遷移に Bias が加わりにくいよう、長い PACE で Bias "
"を追加する。他のいくつかの仮定のもとで、$\\tau = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha_i \\t_i$ "
"となる。ここで、"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:105
msgid "Where $V_i(t)$ is the Metadynamics bias at time $t$."
msgstr "$V_i(t)$ は時刻 $t$ の Metadynamics Bias である。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:107
msgid ""
"$\\alpha_i$ is called the _acceleration factor_ and can be computed by "
"PLUMED for METAD automatically."
msgstr "$\\alpha_i$ は _Acceleration Factor_ と呼ばれ、METAD では PLUMED が自動計算できる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:109
msgid ""
"Note - there are other approaches to take the same data and compute $\\tau$ "
"by fitting the survival distribution, but we will not discuss that here."
msgstr ""
"注意：同じデータから Survival Distribution を Fitting して $\\tau$ を求める方法もあるが、ここでは扱わない。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:111
msgid "Dependence of unbinding rate constants with force"
msgstr "Force に対する Unbinding Rate Constants の依存性"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:113
msgid ""
"For a double well potential, the rate of crossing a high barrier with a "
"small pulling force follows what is sometimes called Bell's law, and is a "
"simple consequence of the linear response of the barrier to pulling:"
msgstr ""
"Double Well Potential では、小さな Pulling Force のもとで高い Barrier を越える Rate は、Bell's"
" Law と呼ばれる関係に従う。これは、Pulling に対して Barrier が Linear Response を示すことから単純に導かれる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:119
msgid ""
"This means that the unbinding accelerates exponentially with force relative "
"to zero force, and this speedup depends on $\\Delta Q^*$ which is the "
"\"distance to the transition state\", assumed to be constant (in reality, "
"this distance moves even for a double well potential, and it is easy to "
"correct for this motion for a quadratic barrier. Feel free to tackle this as"
" an exercise!)."
msgstr ""
"つまり、Force が0の場合に比べ、Unbinding は Force とともに指数関数的に速くなる。この加速は、一定と仮定した Transition"
" State までの距離 $\\Delta Q^*$ に依存する。実際には Double Well でもこの距離は変化するが、Quadratic "
"Barrier なら、その効果を容易に補正できる。演習として取り組んでみてもよい。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:121
msgid "Exercises"
msgstr "演習"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:123
msgid "The exercises are presented below."
msgstr "以下に演習を示す。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:126
msgid "Part 1 - Ligand dissociation from a membrane protein system"
msgstr "パート1：Membrane Protein 系からの Ligand Dissociation"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:128
msgid "System and context"
msgstr "対象系と背景"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:130
msgid ""
"In the following, we illustrate how Plumed can be used to probe ligand-"
"protein interactions in a constant force set-up mimicking single-molecule "
"force spectroscopy experiments that can produce such forces from the pN to "
"the nN regime."
msgstr ""
"以下では、Single-molecule Force Spectroscopy 実験を模倣した Constant Force の設定で、PLUMED "
"を使って Ligand-Protein Interactions を調べる方法を示す。このような実験は pN から nN の Force を発生できる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:132
msgid ""
"On purpuse, we apply it here to a large system comprising the ghrelin "
"receptor GHSR bound to a model peptide GHRP-6 embedded in a model infinite "
"lipid bilayer and solvated by water molecules."
msgstr ""
"ここでは意図的に大きな系を選び、モデル Peptide の GHRP-6 に結合した Ghrelin Receptor GHSR を、無限の Lipid"
" Bilayer のモデルに埋め込み、水分子で溶媒和した系を扱う。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:134
msgid ""
"All input files necessary to run these simulations are available [in this "
"zip file](https://github.com/hockyg/plumed-tutorial-"
"force2/raw/refs/heads/main/plumed-tutorial-force2-data-part1.zip)."
msgstr ""
"これらのシミュレーションに必要な入力ファイルは、[この ZIP ファイル](https://github.com/hockyg/plumed-"
"tutorial-force2/raw/refs/heads/main/plumed-tutorial-force2-data-"
"part1.zip)にすべてある。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:136
msgid ""
"Given the system size, and required simulation lengths, it is highly "
"recommended that you perform these simulations on a cluster, preferably on "
"GPUs. Note that these simulations can be run on a laptop or desktop "
"computer, but you will be limited to quite short simulation times which "
"would require the use of much larger forces to see dissociation within a "
"short time window."
msgstr ""
"系の大きさと必要な計算時間から、可能なら GPU "
"を使い、計算クラスタ上で実行することを強く勧める。ノートPCやデスクトップでも実行できるが、到達できるシミュレーション時間はかなり短い。その時間内に "
"Dissociation を観測するには、はるかに大きな Force が必要になる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:138
msgid ""
"We start by giving a little bit of context on the system chosen for this "
"example, which is shown in the figure below:"
msgstr "まず、この例で選んだ、下図に示す系の背景を簡単に説明する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:140
msgid "![tutorial_figure](images/GHRP-setup.png)."
msgstr "![チュートリアルの系の図](images/GHRP-setup.png)。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:142
msgid ""
"Panel a gives an overview of the system {GHSR+ligand+membrane+water} which "
"is periodic in all directions. In panel b and c, we show the geometric "
"definition of two collective variables, $d_S$ and $d_O$, corresponding "
"respectively to the distance between an anchor in the protein (center of "
"mass between three residues), and either the N-terminal alpha carbon ($d_S$)"
" or the center of mass of the ligand ($d_O$). In plumed, this is achieved as"
" follows:"
msgstr ""
"パネル a は、すべての方向に周期的な系 {GHSR+ligand+membrane+water} の全体像を示す。パネル b と c は、2つの "
"Collective Variables、$d_S$ と $d_O$ の幾何学的定義である。タンパク質内の Anchor（3つの Residues の "
"Center of Mass）から、Ligand の N-terminal α-Carbon（$d_S$）、または Ligand の Center of"
" Mass（$d_O$）までの距離にそれぞれ対応する。PLUMED では次のように実装する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:150
msgid ""
"Please note the NOPBC option that is important here as we don't want to "
"calculate the minimal distance between these two ligand/protein sites."
msgstr "ここでは、Ligand とタンパク質の2点間の最短距離を計算したいわけではないため、NOPBC が重要である。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:152
msgid "Applying a constant, vertical force"
msgstr "一定の垂直な Force を加える"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:154
msgid ""
"Say now that we want to apply a vertical force of a given amplitude to one "
"of these CVs. As detailed in the preambule, this can be achieved by playing "
"with the SLOPE keyword of the RESTRAINT bias. The fact that we apply a "
"vertical force is controlled by the fact that bias is acting on the z "
"component of the CV:"
msgstr ""
"これらの CVs の1つに、指定した大きさの垂直な Force を加えるとする。前述のとおり、RESTRAINT Bias の SLOPE "
"で設定できる。CV の z 成分に Bias を加えることで、Force が垂直になる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:156 INSTRUCTIONS.md:189
msgid "For example,"
msgstr "例えば、"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:171
msgid ""
"Set the energy and distance units to kJ/mol and nm respectively. In these "
"units, what does a SLOPE of -270 correspond to in Piconewtons? (And remember"
" from above, why is the SLOPE defined with a negative sign?)"
msgstr ""
"Energy と距離の単位をそれぞれ kJ/mol と nm に設定する。この単位系で SLOPE が -270 の場合、何 Piconewtons "
"に相当するだろうか。また、前述の内容を踏まえ、なぜ SLOPE に負号を付けるのかを思い出してほしい。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:173
msgid ""
"Make sure to print out the distances you are interested in, as well as the "
"work done by the bias."
msgstr "関心のある距離と、Bias がした Work を出力しておく。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:175
msgid ""
"The amplitude of the force can be tuned by changing the SLOPE, and this can "
"be applied to any CV you like. Steered and constant force MD are by essence "
"out-of-equilibrium set-ups, therefore, if the force is high enough, you "
"don't expect the unbinding event to be reversible. This means that to "
"calculate converged quantities (e.g. unbinding times), you need to repeat "
"such simulations multiple times."
msgstr ""
"SLOPE で Force の大きさを調整でき、任意の CV に適用できる。Steered MD と Constant Force MD "
"は本質的に非平衡の設定なので、Force が十分に大きいと Unbinding は可逆的でないと考えられる。そのため、収束した量、例えば "
"Unbinding Times を求めるには、計算を何度も繰り返す必要がある。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:177
msgid ""
"For example, the following figures shows the results (CV vs time) of 5 "
"trajectories at two different forces acting on $d_S$. How does the unbinding"
" time depends on force? Is this expected?"
msgstr ""
"例えば、次の図は $d_S$ に2種類の Force を加え、それぞれ5つの Trajectories から得た CV "
"の時間変化を示す。Unbinding Time は Force によってどう変わるだろうか。予想どおりだろうか。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:179
msgid "![different_forces](images/GHRP_distance_time.png)."
msgstr "![異なる Forces での結果](images/GHRP_distance_time.png)。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:181
msgid ""
"As an exercise, set SLOPE to match these different conditions. You can "
"either try to play with force further and re-run some simulations to see how"
" this affects the unbinding time, or try to apply force to a different CV, "
"$d_O$ for example."
msgstr ""
"演習として、これらの条件に合うよう SLOPE を設定する。さらに Force を変えて再実行し、Unbinding Time "
"への影響を調べてもよい。また、例えば $d_O$ のような別の CV に Force を加えてもよい。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:183
msgid "Pulling at an angle"
msgstr "角度を付けた Pulling"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:185
msgid ""
"Finally, it could be interesting and relevant for the comparision with "
"experimental results to change the directionnality of the force."
msgstr "最後に、Force の方向を変えることも興味深く、実験結果との比較に役立つ可能性がある。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:187
msgid ""
"For example, providing you with the following plumed file, and based on what"
" we did above, can you guess what is done exactly to the system? Is is "
"expected to have a significant influnce on the unbinding kinetics?"
msgstr ""
"例えば、次の PLUMED ファイルでは、これまでの内容から、系に何をしているかを推測できるだろうか。Unbinding Kinetics "
"に大きく影響すると考えられるだろうか。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:202
msgid ""
"Fill in this PLUMED file so that constant force pulling is performed with a "
"total magnitude of 450 pN at an angle of 40 degrees from the vertical."
msgstr ""
"全体の大きさが450 pN で、垂直方向から40度傾いた Constant Force Pulling を行うよう、PLUMED ファイルを完成させる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:204
msgid ""
"In the following figure, we show the results of the application of a force "
"of 450 pN to the N-terminal residue of the ligand peptide, pulling at "
"different angles. The first angle is that corresponds to the tilt along x, "
"and the second one, if shown, to that along y."
msgstr ""
"次の図は、Ligand Peptide の N-terminal Residue に、異なる角度で450 pN の Force "
"を加えた結果である。最初の角度は x 方向への傾き、2つ目が示されていれば y 方向への傾きを表す。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:206
msgid "![different_angles](images/GHRP_pulling_angles.png)."
msgstr "![異なる角度での結果](images/GHRP_pulling_angles.png)。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:208
msgid "Conclusion"
msgstr "結論"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:210
msgid ""
"You should now be able to apply constant forces to any CV in a given system,"
" and to tune the direction of this force."
msgstr "これで、対象系の任意の CV に Constant Forces を加え、その方向を調整できるはずである。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:212
msgid "Part 2 - Effect of force on unbinding rates"
msgstr "パート2：Unbinding Rates に対する Force の効果"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:214
msgid ""
"In this section, we will demonstrate how unbinding rate constants can be "
"computed via Infrequent Metadynamics. This will be demonstrated on a double-"
"well potential, and simulations will be performed using plumed's `pesmd` "
"function."
msgstr ""
"このセクションでは、Infrequent Metadynamics で Unbinding Rate Constants "
"を求める方法を示す。Double-Well Potential を用い、PLUMED の `pesmd` でシミュレーションする。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:216
msgid ""
"Note, the first step is a recap from the earlier [FISST module "
"masterclass/tutorial](https://www.plumed-"
"tutorials.org/lessons/22/015/data/INSTRUCTIONS.html)."
msgstr ""
"最初の手順は、以前の [FISST の Masterclass／チュートリアル](https://www.plumed-"
"tutorials.org/lessons/22/015/data/INSTRUCTIONS.html)の復習である。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:218
msgid "Reminder - Effect of force on a 2-dimensional potential"
msgstr "復習：2次元 Potential に対する Force の効果"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:220
msgid ""
"Use the RESTRAINT function to add a constant force of different magnitudes "
"(e.g. -2.5 to 2.5 in these units) and look at how the force changes the "
"resulting free energy surface."
msgstr ""
"RESTRAINT を使い、異なる大きさの Constant Force（この単位系では例えば -2.5 から2.5）を加える。Force "
"によって自由エネルギー面がどう変わるかを調べる。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:236
msgid ""
"Then download the [pesmd input file](inputs/doublewell_prod.pesmd.input) run"
" the simulation using the command:"
msgstr ""
"[pesmd 入力](inputs/doublewell_prod.pesmd.input)をダウンロードし、次のコマンドでシミュレーションを実行する。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:242
msgid ""
"Plot the free energy surface from the GRID or after using sum_hills to "
"compute the surface, and zero the potential at the left minimum. What do you"
" notice about the other minimum and barrier?"
msgstr ""
"GRID から、または sum_hills で計算して自由エネルギー面を描く。左側の Minimum で Potential を0に合わせる。もう一方の"
" Minimum と Barrier には、どのような変化が見られるだろうか。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:244
msgid ""
"![Pulling on a double well, sampled by "
"metadynamics](images/doublewell_force_metad.jpg)"
msgstr ""
"![Metadynamics で Sampling した、Double Well に対する Pulling "
"の効果。](images/doublewell_force_metad.jpg)"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:246
msgid "Getting transition times versus force for a double well"
msgstr "Double Well における Transition Times の Force 依存性を求める"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:248
msgid ""
"Perform MetaD using the options below to predict kinetics at different "
"forces. In the figure, we show results from computing the unbinding time "
"using 40 runs with independent seeds for each _pulling_ force from 0 to 1 "
"(be careful with the sign). As you can see, the time goes down "
"exponentially, following Bell's law. Can you extract the transition distance"
" from this figure?"
msgstr ""
"次のオプションで MetaD を実行し、異なる Forces での Kinetics を予測する。図では、0から1の各 _Pulling_ Force "
"について、独立な Seed の計算を40回行って Unbinding Time を求めている。符号に注意すること。Bell's Law "
"に従って時間が指数関数的に減少することが分かる。この図から Transition Distance を求められるだろうか。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:250
msgid ""
"Note that here we used reduced units. An example pesmd input file is given "
"at [this link](inputs/doublewell_rates.pesmd.input)."
msgstr ""
"ここでは Reduced Units を使っている。[こちら](inputs/doublewell_rates.pesmd.input)に pesmd "
"入力の例がある。"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:252
msgid ""
"Fill in the blanks below to turn on calculation of the acceleration factor, "
"and use the COMMITTOR function to stop simulations when the system crosses "
"the barrier (e.g. at x=15.5)"
msgstr ""
"次の空欄を埋めて Acceleration Factor の計算を有効にし、COMMITTOR を使って、系が Barrier を越えたら、例えば "
"x=15.5 でシミュレーションを停止する。"

